  "investigation_notes": "Uploads showed a failure, but the persistent cause is not shown by current evidence.\n\n**What was seen:**\n- `incidentlab-app-1` and `incidentlab-db-1` are both `running` (E1), app `Up 6 seconds`, db `Up 11 seconds (healthy)` at check time.\n- App `/health` returns `200` `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` (E2).\n- App log shows `POST /upload 500 Internal Server Error` with `OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device` in `File \"/app/main.py\", line 59, in upload out.write(chunk)` (E3, E12).\n- Same log (E12) shows before and after that 500:\n  - `POST /upload 200 OK` at `22:33:45.211`, `22:33:45.289`\n  - `POST /upload 500` at `22:33:46.547`\n  - `POST /upload 200 OK` at `22:33:46.798` followed by `GET /files/... 200 OK` and `DELETE /files/... 200 OK`\n  - So failure was single request, not persistent at log end.\n- DB log shows `database system is ready to accept connections` with no error (E8), DB inspect `healthy`, `running true`, `exit_code 0`, `oom_killed false` (E10). App inspect `running true`, `oom_killed false`, `restart_count 0` (E9).\n\n**What was ruled out as persistent root cause:**\n- Host filesystem full: `/srv/lab-data` has `available_mb 410.7`, `use_percent 9.0` (E5). `/` has `available_mb 91431.3`, `use_percent 6.8` (E6). This contradicts `ENOSPC` if it referred to those filesystems at check time.\n- Inode exhaustion: `inode_use_percent 0.2` for `/srv/lab-data` (E5) and `1.5` for `/` (E6).\n- Permission / read-only mount: `/srv/lab-data/uploads` is `0755`, `owner 1000:1000`, `app 1000:1000`, `app_user_can_write true` (E7). `/srv/lab-data` same (E11). App mount is `source /srv/lab-data destination /data rw true` and runs as `1000:1000` (E9). Later successful `POST /upload` + `DELETE` (E12) also proves writability at that time.\n- DB dependency down: DB `healthy` (E1, E10), `pg_isready exit 0` repeated and `health_status: healthy` (E4), DB ready log (E8). App error is `OSError` on `out.write`, not DB connection error (E3, E12).\n- Crash loop: both `restart_count 0`, `running true` (E9, E10). Recent `kill/stop/die/destroy/rename/start` at `22:33:37` (E4) explains short uptime, but containers are up now (E1).\n\n**Conclusion: insufficient_evidence**\n- Symptom confirmed: one upload failed with `ENOSPC` (E3, E12).\n- Root cause not confirmed: host data filesystem and root filesystem both have space and inodes free (E5, E6), permissions allow write (E7, E11, E9), DB is healthy (E1, E8, E10), health is ok (E2), and uploads succeed immediately after the failure (E12).\n- Cannot conclude host disk-full, inode-full, permission-denied, or DB-caused outage. Transient `ENOSPC` could be from a different filesystem, quota, tmpfs, large probe file since deleted, or condition already cleared by recreation at `22:33:37-43` (E4, E9, E10) \u2014 none of which was observed in collected evidence. Repairs should not be approved on disk-full assumption alone; need file-level / quota / in-container `df` at failure time.\n",
